"2100": "Birds survive the cold temperatures of the polar vortex through several adaptations. Firstly, they have specialized feathers that provide excellent insulation, trapping heat close to their bodies. Birds can fluff up these feathers to create a larger insulating layer. Secondly, many birds enter a state called torpor, where their metabolism slows down, reducing energy needs. Additionally, birds may seek shelter in trees, dense shrubs, or burrows. Some species also migrate to warmer areas, while others form groups to share body heat. These combined strategies help them maintain their vital body functions during extreme cold."


  }

